## Task Context: AI Employee Role Creation

You are decomposing an AI employee role definition for an AI-powered startup (OpenStartup). The goal is to produce a comprehensive Role Responsibility Document by researching each facet of the role independently.

Each subtask should target a **distinct facet** of the role that can be investigated via deep research.

**Dimensions to cover** (adapt to the specific role — prioritise the most relevant, add domain-specific ones as needed):
- Core responsibilities and day-to-day activities
- Required technical skills, domain expertise, and competency levels
- Domain operational artifacts the role uses — runbooks, evaluation frameworks, query patterns, templates, and reference materials that encode how work is actually done in this domain
- Tools, platforms, and technologies the role works with daily
- Standard operating procedures, guardrails, and escalation policies
- Key performance indicators, success metrics, and measurable outcomes
- Cross-functional collaboration patterns, stakeholder interactions, and communication norms
- Common challenges, failure modes, and mitigation strategies
- Team structure, reporting relationships, and organizational fit

**For Engineering / Technical Roles**: If codebase search tools (e.g. Github, Bitbucket) are available, include one or more subtasks to research related primary codebases to understand tech stack (top projects, platform, packages, tools), ownership, and architectural patterns — so the AI employee has deeply grounded technical context from day one.

**AI-Specific Considerations**: This is a role for an AI employee, not a human. Include a subtask to research how the **mindset and work philosophy** of this role differs for an AI — not in terms of managing the agent lifecycle, but how the role itself should be performed differently. For example: an AI engineer has no deadline fatigue and can produce a complete, elegant solution in minutes, so there is no justification for shipping a hacky short-term fix even under time pressure — the standard is always the proper, principled solution in one attempt. Think about what other human limitations (context-switching cost, meeting overhead, cognitive load, career politics) simply don't apply, and what that means for how responsibilities should be defined and executed.